<p>The foundation of any successful cross-border move lies in the proper preparation of interstate vehicle transport paperwork, specifically Form 28, the original Registration Certificate (RC), a valid insurance policy, and the Pollution Under Control (PUC) certificate.</p>
<p>Form 28, also known as the No Objection Certificate (NOC), is the most vital document in this process. Issued by the Regional Transport Office (RTO) of the state where the vehicle is currently registered, this form grants official permission for the vehicle to travel to a different state.</p>
<p>Without this specific authorization, law enforcement agencies at state borders may impound the vehicle for violating interstate movement regulations. It is a non-negotiable requirement for anyone utilizing <strong>car transport services</strong> to ensure a smooth transit journey.</p>
<p>Beyond the NOC, the physical transfer of the vehicle requires the original RC copy to prove ownership and legal status. This document must be fully updated and free of any pending fines or dues.</p>
<p>Additionally, a comprehensive insurance policy is mandatory. While standard comprehensive insurance covers accidental damage, specific transport coverage may be required when using a professional <strong>auto transport company</strong> to ensure your vehicle is protected against transit-specific risks.</p>
<p style="font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 12px;">The PUC certificate, or Pollution Under Control certification, is another essential document.</p>
